  "response": "Adjudication between the two accounts begins with the structure of the data brought to bear. Researchers link individual students' test scores across consecutive years to measure gains for the same children. Student-level longitudinal records cannot distinguish compositional change from instructional improvement; this limitation cannot be squared with a design that follows the identical pupils from one year to the next, since holding the roster of tested children fixed is precisely what such linkage accomplishes, and the two characterizations cannot both stand. Federal accountability rules under No Child Left Behind required schools to test at least 95 percent of enrolled students, and classifying low-achieving students as eligible for testing exemptions raises a school's measured average score; a mandatory floor of 95 percent on the share of enrolled children actually tested cannot be squared with an exemption route that lifts the reported average, so these two features of the system cannot both hold.\n\nRetaining low-performing students in an earlier grade removes them from the tested cohort; as a result, cross-sectional comparisons of successive grade cohorts confound instructional improvement with changes in student composition. By contrast, the year-to-year linkage of individual pupils' scores keeps the same children in view and is therefore not exposed to that confounding, which is in any case brought about by the exemption-driven lift in measured averages described above. Retaining low-performing students in grade lowers the average score of the tested grade \u2014 an outcome that cannot be squared with the removal of those students from the tested cohort, because children who are absent from the tested group cannot pull its average down.\n\nExternal benchmarks supply a second line of attack, since a school's own instrument is the very thing in question. The National Assessment of Educational Progress carries no accountability consequences for individual schools. Gains on a state high-stakes test that are absent on the National Assessment of Educational Progress indicate score inflation. The National Assessment of Educational Progress is administered to a representative sample of students. It is precisely because that assessment imposes no consequences on any individual school that a divergence between the two sets of results can be read as inflation rather than as ordinary measurement noise.\n\nAttrition records provide a third source of leverage, because any account resting on composition must show where the missing test-takers went. Rising dropout rates occurring alongside reported score gains are evidence of compositional change. In 2003, Texas state auditors documented the underreporting of dropouts in the Houston Independent School District. Whereas the national assessment reaches a representative sample of the student population, the dropout counts scrutinized by the Texas auditors were those the Houston district itself generated.\n\nWhat ultimately settles the matter is the within-cohort test, which holds the set of children constant and lets the instructional claim stand or fall on its own. The measured gains vanished when the analysis was limited to students enrolled in both years; that is, restricting the sample to continuously enrolled students eliminated the school's apparent improvement. That result leaves the instructional account with nothing left to explain: once the children whose presence in the tested group had changed are set aside, there is no upward movement remaining to be attributed to better teaching, and the compositional reading is the one the evidence sustains.",
